Criteria recorded before reading docs/design/js.md

Sources: worktree docs/spec.md; syntax.md; runtime.md (D2-D5,D9, §§5,7,8,11); host-concurrency.md (ABI v3 and R2 hand-off); meta.md (attribute expansion, compiler-only services). Native note is absent here; read /home/agent/Programming/i/docs/design/native.md revision 2 instead. User principles govern the review.

1. General mechanism, explicit primitive boundary. Ordinary service values obey call-site lookup, substitution, nominal identity and principal inference. Any compiler-defined operation has a defined value-set interpretation and at least three unrelated uses; no namespace-dependent typing or standard-library-only escape hatch. Distinguish a general FFI facility from a target-specific implementation of it. Hard cases: user-defined JS wrapper, fake Js, shadowed names, two same-shaped nominal services, macro-produced host bindings, a fake Target.
2. Host conversion must specify what may cross, validate all inbound values, preserve Int precision, Unicode scalar Strings, finite Floats, bit lengths, identity of opaque references, and ownership. No arbitrary polymorphic service fields or unchecked casts. Hard cases: >2^53 Int, nonfinite JS number, lone surrogate, partial byte, two nominal opaque types, callback/re-entry, JS throw/rejection, closure capturing environment.
3. Standalone artifact must supply every needed import, including runtime imports and initialization faults, with deterministic JS source collection/linking and no accidental globals or undeclared dependencies. Hard cases: unused foreign declaration on other target, two modules with same declaration name, main versus export-only program, top-level initialization stop, JS-specific dependency in portable package, custom host import.
4. WASI replacement must be expressible with the actual ABI. B.2 Int/String are not raw i32/pointer imports; i cannot manipulate linear memory through existing core primitives. Give general raw imports/memory operations or an explicit small target kernel. Hard cases: fd_write iovecs, partial writes, errno, proc_exit, panic before main, panic with fake Io or absent Io. Preserve B.7 stderr+LF/exit1 and §11.7 stack positions.
5. Missing default is an entry requirement check, not a ban on declarations or fake implementations. Service granularity must align with available platform capabilities, not module names. Hard cases: browser entropy without spawn, clock read without sleep, console print without byte stdin, application fallback catching unsupported, runtime permissions versus compile-time absence, a target-dependent branch with both arms type checked.
6. Defaults are ordinary i functions with a specified bootstrap/dependency ordering and capture policy. An entry may get only inferred needs; no change of principal type by target. Hard cases: defaults mutually dependent, async needs hidden behind ordinary wrappers, local root replacement, Target replacement inconsistent with root, effects at initialization, explicit custom host embedding.
7. C ABI v3 and R2 contracts must survive: one root continuation, typed per-request answers, Owner/Await, status2 fairness, synchronous starters, cancellation and provisional-resource cleanup; WASI remains blocking unless separately changed. Hard cases: two concurrent same-import results, cancelled JS promise settling after id reuse, timer alongside runnable task, final awaiting close, completed fake requests in a sync entry.
8. IO resources need a coherent cross-target representation/portability/lifetime rule before IO implementation. Native N9 private nominal Int wrapper plus raw Int ABI versus runtime D9 externref cannot both define File. Hard cases: cross-worker socket, forged raw number, same number in two runs, close with pending request, stop cleanup. Staging must assign changed ABI/default APIs before IO builds them, preserve landed C, use R2 for requests, and distinguish native packaging from multicore work.
